<header><p class="enc-kicker">Event</p><h1 class="enc-title">Maryland Toleration Act</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1649">1649</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maryland">Maryland</a> Toleration Act, passed by the colony's assembly in April 1649, guaranteed freedom of worship to Christians who believed in the Trinity. Promoted by the Catholic proprietor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cecil-calvert">Cecil Calvert</a>, it protected the Catholic minority among a growing Protestant population, though it threatened death for anyone denying the divinity of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesus">Jesus</a>. Repealed in 1654 and later restored, it is an early landmark of religious toleration in America.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/maryland-toleration-act.jpg" alt="Maryland Toleration Act" width="320" height="122" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Assembly of the Province of Maryland · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Large_Broadside_on_the_Maryland_Toleration_Act.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>The Province of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maryland">Maryland</a> was founded in 1634 by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cecil-calvert">Cecil Calvert, 2nd Baron Baltimore</a>, who, like his father <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/george-calvert-1st-baron-baltimore">George Calvert, 1st Baron Baltimore</a>, was a Catholic in a kingdom dominated by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/church-of-england">Church of England</a>. The Calverts meant the colony as a haven for English Catholics and a source of income for their family, but Anglican and Puritan newcomers soon outnumbered the first Catholic settlers. Calvert urged the colonists to leave religious rivalries behind and asked Catholics to practise their faith as privately as possible.</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/english-civil-war">English Civil War</a> opened religious rifts in the colony. In 1647, after the death of Governor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leonard-calvert">Leonard Calvert</a>, Protestants seized control. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cecil-calvert">Cecil Calvert</a> soon regained power but saw that toleration not written into law was vulnerable, and he had also promised freedom of worship to a group of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/puritans">Puritans</a> he induced to settle Providence, now Annapolis. He wrote the act himself, and the assembly, by then dominated by Protestants, passed it on 21 April 1649 at St. Mary's City.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Terms</h2><p>The act, also called the Act Concerning Religion, provided that no one professing to believe in Jesus Christ should be troubled or molested for their religion or in its free exercise. Insulting the Virgin Mary, the apostles or the evangelists could bring whipping, prison or a fine, and calling others heretics was forbidden. Denying the Trinity or the divinity of Jesus could be punished by death or the seizure of land. In 1658 a Jew, Jacob Lumbrozo, was accused of blasphemy under it, but the case was dismissed.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>Some <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/puritans">Puritans</a> and Anglicans opposed the law. In 1652 the parliamentary commissioners William Claiborne and Richard Bennett took over the colonial government, and in 1654 they repealed the act and barred Catholics from openly practising their religion. Calvert regained control by a deal with the colony's Protestants, and the act was passed again in 1657. After the Glorious Revolution, Protestant rebels overthrew Calvert rule and rescinded it in 1692; the colony made the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/church-of-england">Church of England</a> its official church in 1702 and barred Catholics from voting in 1718.</p><p>The act is regarded as the first legal guarantee of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/religious-tolerance">religious tolerance</a> in British North America, and its example may have influenced later measures such as the British Act of Toleration of 1689 and the Holy Experiment in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pennsylvania">Pennsylvania</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-amendment-to-the-united-states-constitution">First Amendment to the United States Constitution</a> echoes its phrase &quot;free exercise thereof&quot;. Rhode Island, however, had passed laws against religious persecution from 1636.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Maryland Toleration Act” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Catholics remained a minority, and the <b>Toleration Act</b> of 1649 protected Trinitarian Christians.</blockquote><a href="/state/province-of-maryland">Province of Maryland</a></li></ul></section>
